## Solution

For this challenge, I followed the suggested
solution from Chapter 25.

I think Lox should create a separate captured
variable for every iteration of a for loop.

This behavior is useful because a closure created
during one iteration should be able to remember
the value from that particular iteration.

If all closures share the same variable, they
may instead observe its final value after the
loop finishes.

To implement the change, I would modify
forStatement() in compiler.c to introduce a
new local scope and a shadowing variable for
each iteration.

## 1. Understanding the Original Behavior

Consider this Lox program:

```lox
var first = nil;
var second = nil;

fun main() {
  for (var i = 1; i <= 2; i = i + 1) {
    fun remember() {
      print i;
    }

    if (first == nil) {
      first = remember;
    } else {
      second = remember;
    }
  }
}

main();

first();
second();
```

In the original Chapter 25 implementation,
the loop variable i is declared once.

Both closures capture the same variable.

The loop also increments i after the second
iteration, so its final value is 3.

Therefore, the original behavior is:

```text
3
3
```

For my proposed design, I would prefer:

```text
1
2
```

Each closure would capture a separate variable
created during its own iteration.

This would make the behavior more intuitive
when closures are created inside loops.

## 2. Create a Shadowing Variable for Each Iteration

The suggested solution uses a new scope inside
the loop body.

The original loop variable continues to control
the condition and increment expressions.

However, each iteration gets a temporary local
variable with the same name.

The new variable begins with the current value
of the original loop variable.

When the compiler processes the loop body,
references to that name resolve to the new
inner variable instead of the original one.

This means closures created during that
iteration capture the temporary variable.

At the end of the iteration, the compiler
copies the temporary variable's value back
to the original loop variable.

This step is important because the loop body
might modify the variable.

Finally, the temporary scope is closed.

If the temporary variable was captured by
a closure, Chapter 25's existing upvalue
mechanism preserves its value after the
iteration finishes.

## 3. Modify forStatement()

The following proposed implementation follows
the uploaded solution.

In compiler.c:

```c
static void forStatement() {
  beginScope();

  // Track the loop variable's name and stack slot.
  int loopVariable = -1;
  Token loopVariableName;
  loopVariableName.start = NULL;

  consume(TOKEN_LEFT_PAREN,
          "Expect '(' after 'for'.");

  if (match(TOKEN_VAR)) {
    // Remember the name of the declared variable.
    loopVariableName = parser.current;

    varDeclaration();

    // Remember its local stack slot.
    loopVariable = current->localCount - 1;

  } else if (match(TOKEN_SEMICOLON)) {
    // No initializer.

  } else {
    expressionStatement();
  }

  int loopStart = currentChunk()->count;

  int exitJump = -1;

  if (!match(TOKEN_SEMICOLON)) {
    expression();

    consume(TOKEN_SEMICOLON,
            "Expect ';' after loop condition.");

    exitJump = emitJump(OP_JUMP_IF_FALSE);
    emitByte(OP_POP);
  }

  if (!match(TOKEN_RIGHT_PAREN)) {
    int bodyJump = emitJump(OP_JUMP);

    int incrementStart = currentChunk()->count;

    expression();
    emitByte(OP_POP);

    consume(TOKEN_RIGHT_PAREN,
            "Expect ')' after for clauses.");

    emitLoop(loopStart);
    loopStart = incrementStart;

    patchJump(bodyJump);
  }

  // Create a separate variable for this iteration.
  int innerVariable = -1;

  if (loopVariable != -1) {
    beginScope();

    // Initialize the inner variable using the
    // current value of the outer loop variable.
    emitBytes(OP_GET_LOCAL, (uint8_t)loopVariable);

    // Declare a variable with the same name.
    addLocal(loopVariableName);
    markInitialized();

    innerVariable = current->localCount - 1;
  }

  // Compile the loop body.
  statement();

  if (loopVariable != -1) {
    // Copy changes made in the body back to
    // the original loop variable.
    emitBytes(OP_GET_LOCAL, (uint8_t)innerVariable);
    emitBytes(OP_SET_LOCAL, (uint8_t)loopVariable);
    emitByte(OP_POP);

    // Close the per-iteration variable.
    endScope();
  }

  emitLoop(loopStart);

  if (exitJump != -1) {
    patchJump(exitJump);
    emitByte(OP_POP);
  }

  endScope();
}
```

## 4. Why Shadowing Works

The compiler resolves local variables by
searching the most recently declared locals
first.

When the new inner variable has the same
name as the original loop variable, it
shadows the original one.

For example:

```lox
for (var i = 1; i <= 2; i = i + 1) {
  fun show() {
    print i;
  }
}
```

During the first iteration, the body uses
a temporary variable initialized to 1.

During the second iteration, it uses a
new temporary variable initialized to 2.

The closure from each iteration captures
its own variable instead of capturing
the original loop-control variable.

This prevents the closures from all
sharing the final value of i.

## 5. Preserving Changes Made Inside the Loop

A separate variable must not interfere
with normal loop behavior.

Consider:

```lox
for (var i = 0; i < 3; ) {
  print i;
  i = i + 1;
}
```

Expected output:

```text
0
1
2
```

This loop has no increment clause.

Instead, the body updates i directly.

If the compiler only created a temporary
shadowing variable without copying its
value back, the original loop variable
would remain zero.

The loop condition would continue to
evaluate using the unchanged value,
potentially causing an infinite loop.

The proposed solution avoids this by
copying the inner variable's value
back to the original loop variable
before finishing the iteration.

The original variable then controls
the next condition or increment.

## 6. Closing the Temporary Scope

Chapter 25 introduces upvalues to allow
closures to access local variables even
after the declaring scope ends.

When the temporary scope finishes,
endScope() checks whether its local
variable was captured.

If the variable was not captured,
the compiler can emit OP_POP.

If the variable was captured,
the compiler emits OP_CLOSE_UPVALUE.

This moves the captured value into
the upvalue object so the closure
can continue accessing it.

As a result, a closure can retain its
iteration-specific variable even after
the loop has moved on.

## 7. Test 1: Two Different Captured Values

```lox
var first = nil;
var second = nil;

fun main() {
  for (var i = 1; i <= 2; i = i + 1) {
    fun remember() {
      print i;
    }

    if (first == nil) {
      first = remember;
    } else {
      second = remember;
    }
  }
}

main();

first();
second();
```

Expected output after the proposed change:

```text
1
2
```

Each closure should retain the
value associated with the iteration
during which it was created.

## 8. Test 2: Modifying the Loop Variable

```lox
for (var i = 0; i < 3; ) {
  print i;
  i = i + 1;
}
```

Expected output:

```text
0
1
2
```

This verifies that modifications made
to the shadowing variable are copied
back into the original loop variable.

## 9. Test 3: Ordinary Loop Behavior

```lox
for (var i = 0; i < 4; i = i + 1) {
  print i;
}
```

Expected output:

```text
0
1
2
3
```

This verifies that an ordinary for loop
still behaves as expected even when
there are no closures in its body.

## 10. Trade-Offs

The main benefit of this approach is
that closures created during different
iterations retain independent variables.

This can make programs easier to
understand and help prevent confusing
results when functions are created
inside loops.

The main disadvantage is that the
compiler must generate additional
instructions and manage an extra scope
for each iteration of a for loop that
declares a local variable.

The implementation also needs to
handle assignments to the loop variable
correctly so the loop can still advance.

Although the implementation becomes
more complicated, I think the behavior
is worth it because it is often what
a programmer expects when capturing
variables inside loops.

## 11. Compatibility With Other Loop Features

This implementation is based on the
Chapter 25 version of forStatement().

If the continue statement from
Chapter 23's optional challenge has
also been implemented, its compilation
must be adjusted to account for the
new per-iteration scope.

A continue statement must preserve any
required updates to the original loop
variable and close captured temporary
locals before jumping to the loop's
increment or condition.

Simply combining the two versions of
forStatement() without coordinating
their control-flow and scope handling
may result in incorrect behavior.
